# Normalize line endings. Without this, what lands in a commit depends on each
# contributor's core.autocrlf, so the same file can flip between LF and CRLF
# between machines and show up as a whole-file diff that isn't a real change.
#
# Text is stored LF in the repository and checked out LF, matching the sibling
# consuming repo (blueprintnotincluded). Visual Studio, MSBuild and PowerShell
# all read LF fine on Windows.
#
# This codifies what the repository already stored: every tracked text file was
# LF in the index before this file existed, so adding it renormalized nothing
# and produced no churn. Verify with `git ls-files --eol`.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Visual Studio rewrites solution files with CRLF whenever it touches them, so
# forcing LF here just produces churn every time the solution is opened.
*.sln text eol=crlf

# Explicitly binary — never diffed, never line-ending converted.
*.dll binary
*.nupkg binary
*.pdb binary
*.snk binary
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.zip binary
